\import{spin-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{connes}
\tag{lean}
\tag{property-t}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Property (T) as a representation interface}

\p{For a countable discrete group #{G}, a unitary representation #{\pi} has
\newvocab{almost-invariant unit vectors} when every finite #{K\subset G} and
every #{\varepsilon>0} admit a unit vector #{\xi} with

##{\lVert \pi(g)\xi-\xi\rVert<\varepsilon\qquad(g\in K).}

The project defines property (T) by requiring every such representation to
contain a nonzero invariant vector. The exact Lean boundary is
\href{https://github.com/utensil/connes-rigidity/blob/main/Connes/Core.lean#L46-L88}{\code{UnitaryRepresentation.HasAlmostInvariantUnitVectors}, \code{HasKazhdanPropertyT}, and \code{HasRelativePropertyT}}.
This qualitative form matches the final consumer, while quantitative Kazhdan
sets and constants remain local to any future proof of the EJZK input.}

\p{The useful decomposition is the fixed subspace #{H^N} of a normal subgroup
#{N} and its orthogonal complement. Relative property (T) forces a nonzero
vector into #{H^N}; property (T) of the quotient then acts on #{H^N}. The
formal theorem
\href{https://github.com/utensil/connes-rigidity/blob/main/Connes/Foundation/OperatorAlgebra/PropertyTTransfer.lean#L264-L278}{\code{hasKazhdanPropertyT_of_relative_and_quotient}}
packages this argument. Isolating it prevents the later spectral proof from
depending on the concrete presentation of Zhou's groups.}

\card{Engineering note}{Quantitative facts should discharge a qualitative boundary}{
\p{A future EJZK development should expose Kazhdan pairs, ratios, or spectral
gaps internally, then export exactly \code{HasKazhdanPropertyT elementaryGroup}.
Changing the public theorem to demand a particular constant would strengthen
the paper's hypothesis and couple every consumer to an arbitrary estimate.}}
